  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 145 PM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nWeak southwesterly upper-level flow is anticipated for Saturday \nwith lax pressure gradients between troughing over the Pacific \nnorthwest and a ridge to the southeast. The majority of area \nwill be capped through the early afternoon. A weak upper level\nshortwave would promote a few morning showers and general\ncloudiness before moving through in the early afternoon. A few \nshowers and thunderstorms may be possible in the mountains and along I-25\nearly on with a few storms developing in the NE panhandle areas\nlater on. A storm or two may be marginally severe, though\nlimited bulk shear (25-35 kt) and CAPE (1000-1500 J/kg) will\nkeep most activity on the weaker side. Pwat values are slightly\nelevated compared to climate average due to some monsoonal flow, \nso, storms may be better able to produce beneficial rain were \nthey occur. Outside of storms afternoon high temperatures are\nexpected to make it into the 90s east of I-25 and 80s to the\nwest. Minimum relative humidity values in the low 20s and upper\nteens could lend itself to elevated fire potential though overnight\nrecoveries are good.\n\nSunday is much the same as Monday. The trough over the Pacific NW\nwill inch closer to our area allowing for slightly better forcing\nand advection of monsoon. Therefore, the odds of storms increase.\nThe forecasted PoPs are about 40-60% area wide, while model ensembles\nare a little more giving with 50-75% chances of rain. PW values\nof an inch to 1.5 over southeast Wyoming and the Nebraska\nPanhandle may lead to a sneaky flooding potential. Corfidi\nvectors are in the 15 to 20 knot range with Bunkers right motion\nbeing nearly stationary. These parameters are sensitive to details\nthat are hard to resolve at this range, but the chance of slow\nmoving and heavy rainfall producing storms is possible.\n\nThe trend of showers and thunderstorms continue through Monday\nand Tuesday as the stubborn trough to the northwest finally\nejects into the central plains. While moisture will remain\nhigh, a lobe of drier air associated with a ridge to the east\nmay be pulled in ahead of the trough which would reduce rain\npotential. However, since the trough is ejecting it is possible\nthat cyclogenesis occurs nearby increasing the severe weather\npotential. Some machine learning models are highlighting an\nuptick in that potential for the Nebraska Panhandle area.\nThe details of how this unfolds will be ironed out as we get\ncloser.\n\nThe remainder of the long term is in a drying trend. The\nridge noses in. This would not completely shut down storm\nactivity.",
